## 2.2. Mixing of More than One Metal-Containing SBU
MOFs having different types of inorganic secondary building unit (SBU) have been prepared by employing two types of metal ion during the synthesis.[18] For example, when a series of lanthanide metals ( $\mathbf{\tilde{L}n}{}=\mathbf{Pr}$ , Gd, and Er) were treated with a Mn salt source and pyridine-2,6-dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}D I P I C\rangle}$ , the resulting MOFs $\mathrm{\small{[Ln(DIPIC)_{3}M n_{1.5}-}}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{3}]$ showed enneatopic and tritopic metal SBUs.[19] Similarly, by employing In and $Z\mathrm{r}$ metals, a mixed-coordination metal–imidazolate framework, ZIF-5 $[\mathrm{In}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{Im})_{12}]$ was obtained, where $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ are in tetrahedral and octahedral coordination geometries, respectively.[20]
One of the first examples of a MOF constructed from a single metal ion, yet containing different SBUs, was reported in 2005, USF-4 $[\mathrm{Zn}_{6}(\mathrm{BTC)_{4}(i s o q u i n o l i n e)_{4}}]$ - $(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}]$ ( $\mathbf{BTC}=$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate).[21] In this structure, tritopic BTC linkers were connected with dinuclear square and tetrahedral inorganic $Z\mathrm{n}$ SBUs. Two types of inorganic SBU were also introduced through the use of a tritopic linker (biphenyl-3,4’,5-tricarboxylate, BPTC): UMCM-150 $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B P T C)_{2}]}$ is composed of $\mathrm{Cu}$ paddle-wheel and trinuclear trigonal-prism $\mathtt{C u}$ SBUs.[22] The resulting material has a high Langmuir surface area $(3100\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity $5.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 45 bar). More recently, a hexatopic linker with four, three-coordinated branching points [trimethyl substituted $3,3^{\prime},3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime},5^{\prime\prime}$ -benzene-1,3,5-triylhexabenzoate (TMBHB)] was employed to obtain a tfe-b net possessing dinuclear triangular and square SBUs, $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{7}(T M B H B)_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}]}}$ (Figure 4 a).[23] Another approach for designing MOFs with two kinds of inorganic SBU involves the utilization of organic linkers with two different terminal functional groups, such as 5-tetrazolylisophthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}T Z I)}$ .[24] In this case, carboxylate terminals of TZI linker form metal–organic polyhedron1 (MOP-1, $[\mathrm{Cu}_{24}(m\mathrm{BDC})_{24}]$ ; $m{\bf B}{\bf D}{\bf C}=$ isophthalate)[25] like cages with $\mathtt{C u}$ paddle-wheels, which are connected through terminal tetrazole units $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}O_{4}(-C N_{4})_{3}]}$ (Figure 4b). Overall, the structure can be described as a (3,24)-connected rht net $[\mathrm{Cu}_{6}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{TZI})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{9}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})]$ .
2.3. Mixing Multiple Inorganic SBUs and More than One Organic Linker within the Same MOF
By employing more than two types of organic and/or inorganic SBU, a more complex framework was obtained in one-pot reactions. In 2011, a MOF having a cagewithin-cage structure, CPM-24 $\{[\mathrm{Co_{9}(O H)}_{2}$ (acetate)- $\mathrm{(BTC)_{4}(I N)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ $[\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}}]_{5}$ ( $\mathbf{IN}=$ isonicotinate)}, was reported (Figure 5);[26] each larger rhombicuboctahedral cage, composed of $\mathbf{Co}$ binuclear units $\mathrm{[Co_{48}(I N)_{48}]}$ , contains smaller cuboctahedral cages with Co paddlewheel and BTC linkers $\mathrm{[Co_{24}(B T C)_{24}]}$ (Figure 5 b). Furthermore, IN is coordinated to the outer surface of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ paddle-wheels, in which four of these IN form another $\mathrm{Co}$ paddle-wheel unit (Figure 5c). CPM-24 showed high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $(68.4\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 atm and 273 K), but negligible ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake under the same conditions; therefore, high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selective adsorption was expected (calculated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity $=106{:}1$ at $0.16\mathrm{atm}^{\cdot}$ ).
To design desirable structures with many types of organic   
linkers and metal ions, molecular building blocks can be   
assembled through two-step reactions (Figure 6).[27] In this   
example, in the first step, $\mathrm{Cr}$ trigonal prism SBUs $\{\lbrack\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O(H-}$ $\mathrm{IN})_{4}(\mathrm{IN})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}](\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{5}\}$ were prepared. Since the terminal sites of these $\mathrm{Cr}$ SBUs were decorated with pyridine units, the building blocks were easily bound to $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and tritopic organic linkers to form $\{\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{3}\mathbf{-}$ $\mathrm{(BTC)_{2}[C r_{3}O(I N)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{2}(O H)]\}$ (tp-PMBB-1-asc-1). Expanded derivatives of trinodal MOFs (i.e. containing triangular, tetrahedral, and trigonal prismatic SBUs) were also obtained by employing larger tritopic organic linkers.[27] The presence of permanent microporosity for tp-PMBB-1-asc-1 was demonstrated by ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherm measurements on the guest-free form of the material (BET surface area $\mathsf{\Omega}_{1}=1671\mathrm{\m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . This material also showed enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $(136\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ at 1 atm and $273\mathrm{K}$ compared to that of CPM-24.[26]
## 2.4. Mixing of Multiple Functional Groups along the Backbone
Another strategy for designing heterogeneity within MOFs is to incorporate two or more types of functionality in a MOF framework. The challenge presented is how to mix two or more organic linkers of the same type, each having unique functionality, without producing mixed phases rather than a single, pure MOF phase with varying functionalities. In 2010, multivariate MOFs
(MTV-MOFs), where multiple organic functionalities were incorporated into a MOF-5 $[\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(B D C)_{3}}]^{[28]}$ structure, were reported.[7] One of the MTV-MOF-5 materials contains eight different functionalized BDC linkers (X-BDC, $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ,
Br, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , $(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{{C_{4}H_{4}}}$ , $(\mathrm{OC}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{2}$ , and, $\mathrm{(OC,H_{7})_{2})}$ within its structure without losing either the parent MOF-5 backbone structure, crystallinity, or microporosity (Figure 7 a). The arrangement of organic linkers in the MOF structure was not fully elucidated; however, through solid-state NMR spectral analysis as well as molecular dynamics simulations, an apportionment of organic functionalities within the MTV-MOF structure was found depending on the type of functionality used. For example, it was observed that $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and $\left(\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\right)_{2}$ functionalized linkers tended to form small clusters within the MTV-MOF, whereas - $\mathbf{\cdotNO}_{2}$ , $(\mathrm{-OC}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{2}$ , and $(\mathrm{-OC}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{7})_{2}$ functionalized linkers were well-mixed throughout the framework.[8] The MTV material had an $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity improved by $400\%$ compared to the parent MOF-5 architecture, and this trend was reproduced through simulation calculations.[7] The plausible explanation for this observed high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity is that MTV materials have both large numbers of adsorption sites and sufficient pore space. Such nonlinear properties are also reported for MTV compounds, which show higher surface areas than those measured for the corresponding MOFs constructed from a single organic linker.[29]
In contrast to MTV-MOFs containing various kinds of organic functionalities, the history of MOFs containing two or more different metal ions is more extensive. In 1992, $\mathrm{{N}(C_{4}H_{9})_{4}[M C r(o x a l a t e)_{3}]}$ $\mathbf{\Delta}^{'}\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{Z\ n^{2+}}$ type structures were synthesized by a one-pot reaction.[30] These materials have magnetic features characteristic of ferromagnets; however, their porous nature was not studied, perhaps because of the presence of bulky $\mathrm{NBu}_{4}$ counter cations in the framework. Recently, it was reported that Co-doped MOF-5 structures could be prepared through a one-pot synthesis, in which $8\%$ or $21\%$ of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions were replaced with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions, respectively.[31] From the detailed analysis of these materials, it is presumed that ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions were incorporated into the framework; however, the precise location of these doped metal ions was not clear. Even more recently, a microcrystalline MOF-74 $\left[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{DOT})\right]$ 1 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{DOT}=}$ dioxidoterephthalate)[32] structure was obtained, through a one-pot reaction, containing up to 10 different kinds of divalent metals (Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Zn, and Cd) within the structure (Figure 7 b).[33] The distribution of metal ions in the crystalline powder samples was studied by energy-dispersive $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray spectroscopy, which indicated that the distribution of the metal ions might not be uniform.
Mixed-metal MOFs were also obtained by post-synthetic metal exchange reactions. For instance, $98\%$ of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions in a Cd-MOF $\{\mathrm{Cd_{1.5}(H_{3}O)_{3}[(C d_{4}O)_{3}(H E T T)_{8}]\cdot6H_{2}O\}}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{HETT}=5,5^{\prime},10,10^{\prime},15,15^{\prime}$ -hexaethayltruxene-2,7,12-tricarboxylic acid) were replaced by $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions through immersing the MOF in a $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ solution.[34] Single-crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction analyses of these materials revealed that the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{d}$ and $\mathrm{\bf{Pb}}$ ) distances are different from one another other, thus making it reasonable to conclude that the ionexchange process occurred without loss of the materials crystallinity. $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions in MOF-5 have also been exchanged with metal ions $\mathrm{((Cl)Ti^{n}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaV^{II}}$ , $(\mathrm{Cl})\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\mathrm{Cr^{II},M n^{I I}}$ , or $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ) that could not otherwise be incorporated into the framework through a direct synthesis route.[35] It could be shown that when the metal ions were introduced into the MOF-5 framework with reduced oxidation states, they could be subsequently oxidized without changing the framework structure (transformation from $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ to $(\mathrm{BF}_{4})\mathrm{Cr}-$ MOF-5 by exposure to $\mathrm{NOBF_{4}}$ in acetonitrile).
## 2.5. MOFs with Random and Ordered Defects
Introduction of defect sites into ordered structures is also a useful strategy to realize unusual heterogeneous structures and properties, even if it is a big challenge to elucidate how the defect sites are distributed in the framework.[36] One of the easiest methods to introduce such sites is by post-synthetic acid treatment.[37] Brønsted acid sites have been introduced into MIL-100(Fe) $\mathrm{[Fe_{3}O F(H_{2}O)_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ , without structural or porosity damage, by immersing the material in an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}$ or $\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$ . Although the local structure is unclear, it is likely that extra open metal sites were created by the acid treatment.[37,38]
Another method for incorporating defects is to utilize modulator molecules, such as acetic acid and formic acid.[39,40] When modulator molecules are added during the MOF synthesis, they can be incorporated into the framework to create heterogeneity (as a result of missing organic linkers) even after sample activation. Through the addition of trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) within the appropriate reaction mixture to synthesize UiO-66 $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(B D C)_{6}]}$ , it was found that some of the BDC linkers were replaced with trifluoroacetate.[39] The resulting structure showed enhanced catalytic activity in the cyclization of citronellal to isopulegol. The presence of defects was also confirmed by $\mathbf{\deltaX}$ -ray diffraction measurements.[40]
The third strategy for introducing this type of heterogeneity is by rapid precipitation of MOF.[41] Although conventional solvothermal synthesis requires anywhere from $12\mathrm{{h}}$ to several days to yield single crystal samples, it is expected that defect sites within a MOF structure will result unavoidably from fast crystal formation $(<1\mathrm{min})$ .[42] To investigate whether Brønsted -OH groups $\scriptstyle\sum\mathrm{n-OH}$ species) can be introduced, MOF-5 was prepared by a room-temperature synthesis with and without 2-methyltoluic acid (modulator).[41] Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) data implied the presence of -OH functionalities; however, considering the products low crystallinity and surface area, it may be likely that the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -OH moiety was not introduced into the MOF-5 structure.
Perhaps the most common strategy in the creation of defect sites is by co-assembly of metal ion, linker, and fragment of the parent linker.[36,43,44] One of the early examples reported was PCN-125 $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{TPTC})\right]$ $\mathrm{{TPTC=}}$ $1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate), which is composed of $\mathrm{cu}$ paddle-wheel units connected by tetratopic TPTC linkers.[43] Since mBDC is a fragment of the TPTC linker in PCN-125, functionalized R-mBDC ( $\mathbf{R}=\mathbf{H}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{\SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaSO_{3}H}$ , or $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{3},$ ) linkers were added to the reaction mixture used to synthesize PCN-125. Such MOF materials displayed identical powder X-ray diffraction patterns to the original PCN-125 and solutionstate NMR analyses (based on digested MOF samples) confirmed the presence of R-mBDC linkers within the structures. This synthetic approach was in sharp contrast to those of other mixed-linker systems, such as UMCM-1 and MOF-210, which yield MOFs with complex topologies.[12,13] It is noted that the pore diameters of several of these defective MOFs increased when shorter linkers were incorporated. In effect, this strategy can enable both pore expansion and functionalization. Indeed, several of these MOFs containing linker fragments showed improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake when compared to the pristine PCN-125.
A similar strategy was also applied to HKUST-1 $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\cdot\right.$ $\mathbf{\left(BTC\right)}_{2}]^{[45]}$ (Figure 8 a),[36,44] where the tritopic BTC linkers were partially replaced with ditopic mBDC linkers, such as mBDC, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -mBDC, CN-mBDC, OH-mBDC, 3,5-pyridinedicarboxylate (PDC). To investigate the distribution of defect sites, defect-engineered MOFs (DEMOFs; $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{(2-x)}-}$ (ditopic linker)x]) were prepared by varying the ratio of the tritopic BTC and ditopic $m$ BDC linkers (Figure 8 b).[36] FT-IR analysis of HKUST-1 indicated that new open metal sites were formed upon addition of ditopic mBDC linkers and the degree of defects increased in order of $\mathbf{NO}_{2^{-}}m\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}<\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N}.$ $m{\bf B}{\bf D}C<{\bf O}{\bf H}{\bf-}m{\bf B}{\bf D}C<{\bf P}{\bf D}C$ . Furthermore, it was possible to create mesopores by implementing this defect-engineering strategy. In this case, since the defect sites can change the local structure of the Cu paddle-wheel units, it was expected that the physical properties of such MOFs, including band gap and magnetism, could be finely tuned. Note that removal of the Cu paddle-wheel unit from HKUST-1 can also create defect sites (Figure 8 c).[44] In this scenario, defective MOFs display greater surface area than their corresponding parent MOF structures.
A recent report showed that it is possible to generate vacancies in MOFs in an ordered manner to produce ordered vacancies. A quarter of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions and half of the pyrazolecarboxylate (PyC) linkers were removed from a cubic MOF $\mathrm{[Zn_{4}O(P y C)_{3}]}$ (Figure 9 a) to create ordered controlling the level of interpenetration. In 2012, a partially interpenetrated MOF NOTT-202 $\{(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{1.75}[\mathrm{In(BTPC)}]_{1.75}\}$ 1 $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}H_{4}B T P C=}$ biphenyl$^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetra-(phenyl-4-carboxylic acid) was synthesized.[49] The first net (net A) has a binodal diamondoid topology composed of $\mathrm{In}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions and BTPC linkers. The second net (net B) was shown to be two disordered diamond nets, but the occupancy of both nets was 0.375. Therefore, it was concluded that the overall structure could be described as a partially interpenetrating framework (net A:net $\mathbf{B}=1{:}0.75\$ ). Interestingly, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ isotherm at $195\mathrm{K}$ of NOTT-202 exhibited a distinctive step with a significant hysteresis loop, whereas at higher temperatures (over $212\mathrm{K}$ ), this step was not observed. The plausible explanation for this is that there was stepwise filling of the pores as a result of structural defect sites generated by the partial interpenetration present.
